> Text:
> Introduction
> 
> The impacts of climate change are incredibly complex. The human and natural systems of the future will have fundamentally shifted in unpredictable ways, and their evolution will be partly shaped by how they are impacted by and respond to climate risk. Climate impacts emerge from unexpected weather shocks, long-term climate trends, and local interactions, while the globally correlated nature of climate change disrupts systems at all scales. Climate risks interact across space, time, and sectors, resulting in cascading, concurrent, and systemic risks.
> 
> The scientific methods that have emerged to understand climate risks struggle with this task. Generally these tools fall into three groups, and their differing underlying principles are central to the contribution offered by PANACEA.
> Biophysical process models have long histories in areas like agriculture (Jones et al. 2017) and flooding (Wing et al. 2024). Built by natural scientists and engineers, these models represent the dynamic processes behind outcomes of interest, such as soil water balance and hydrology. While very sophisticated, these models have significant challenges in describing existing arrangements and economic realities (Lobell et al. 2009).
> Econometric statistical approaches have emerged in many areas of climate risks, such as health and economic growth (Carleton & Hsiang 2016). Econometrics extends multivariate regression with techniques and principles that aim to distinguish "causal" from "correlative" relationships. However, it is unclear when their results can be extrapolated out-of-sample, since they assume a level of stationarity that is difficult to justify under climate change (Hsiang 2016, Rising et al. 2022a).
> Macroeconomic models, such as partial equilibrium models that study how supply and demand shape market forces and shifting land use (e.g., GLOBIOM), have played an important role to synthesise impacts across an economy. These represent market relationships at a given point in time, but assume that impacts do not result in changes to the structure of the economy itself (Piontek et al. 2021).
> 
> Our understanding of climate risks remains siloed, with relatively independent literatures around these three approaches. These parallel activities are no longer adequately addressing societal goals, particularly due to the rising importance of adaptation, the need to better understand non-marginal changes in the economy and tipping points in the climate, and the interactions between climate risks, adaptation, development, and mitigation.
> 
> The central scientific question of PANACEA is, how can integrating across these methodologies improve our understanding of climate risks, adaptation investments, and the interaction between climate damages, mitigation, and development?
> 
> The key insight is that these three approaches have complementary strengths and operate at different, nested scales. Biophysical process models capture interactions within the natural system, but assume exogenously-determined management practices, e.g., for agriculture, forestry, flooding and water resources, human health, and labour productivity. However, people adjust their behaviour dynamically, and econometric models identify parameters that describe the net outcome of these human-natural feedbacks (Piontek et al. 2021). Finally, while econometric models describe outcomes under historical supply-demand, land use, and policy regimes, macroeconomic models recognise that these are themselves subject to endogenous evolution. Each approach also captures a different dimension of adaptation. Biophysical process models typically include management-based adaptation; econometric models capture autonomous adaptation; and macroeconomic models capture large-scale redistribution of activity.  Figure 1 shows these nested structures for two of the impact cases further explored in PANACEA.
> 
> 
> Figure 1. Example of the nesting of agricultural biophysical, econometric, and macroeconomic models. Crop models capture interactions between plans and the environment, while statistical yield models include the role of dynamic management. Macroeconomic models, as used in food supply and demand models, capture further dynamic futures of the economy and space.
