> PANACEA proposes four interconnected projects that elaborate different ways to address the challenge of improving climate risk and adaptation information. All of the approaches exploit this nested model structure, and together they address plausibly the most important and challenging climate risks at a global level. The projects address the following questions:
> How are countries exposed to climate risks occurring across their borders? Which supply chains are most in need of improved resilience?
> What portion of recent and projected rise in flooding deaths, displacement, and damages is attributable to changes in land use and local population growth (e.g., the periurban margin in developing countries)? What role can policies that curtail the expansion of residential development in risky areas have?
> How vulnerable are major perennial crop production to future yield shocks and plant mortality, with a focus on coffee, tea, and cocoa? What kinds of improvements in management practices have the most benefit?
> Which species and species groups are most at risk of local and global extinction? Where can protected areas provide long-term benefits, accounting for emerging risks?
> 
> Across all of these, short-comings in the current capability of data-driven methods and the resulting gap in the evidence base are central. PANACEA draws on multiple modeling approaches to facilitate a sea-change in the robustness and applicability of econometric methods for complex climate risks. Econometrics has revolutionised our understanding of many risks, partly because they better capture consequences of weather risks today, but they are also susceptible to various forms of misuse (Rising et al. 2024). PANACEA focuses on the interdisciplinary "glue" that can offer a firmer foundation for future risk estimates and a more flexible representation of adaptation (see Figure 2). New techniques in machine learning and computational Bayesian methods make this progress possible.
> 
> Figure 2: The four channel-specific projects and methodologies to bridge gaps. The four projects are reported in Table 1. Machine Learning and Computational Bayesian methods allow an integration between Macroeconomic models, Econometrics, and Biophysical process models.
> 
> The key evidence gaps for each channel are summarised in Table 1. 
> 
> Impact Channel
> Evidence Gap
> R1. GDP Loss & Supply Chains
> Econometric estimates of GDP growth, which have opened up a crucial line of evidence on macroeconomic risks in recent years, structurally ignore the role of correlated foreign shocks (Change et al. 2023).
> R2. River & Surface Flooding
> Flood impacts are almost exclusively studied with process-based approaches, and local calibration of flood risk models is very data intensive (Winsemius et al. 2016).
> R3. Perennial Crops
> Perennial crops are understudied, due to lack of data on management choices and pervasive misreporting of yields (Rising 2024).
> R4. Biodiversity Loss
> Global studies of species distributions generally use simplistic suitability envelopes (Frans et al. 2024). Evidence from many species points to weather shocks as a significant driver of local extinction (Kimmel et al. 2022, Nikolaou & Katsanevakis 2023).
> 
> Table 1. Specific impact channels studied within PANACEA, the evidence gap that these studies are meant to address.
